"""Original identity and access scenarios."""
ITEMS=[]
def group(options,rows):
 for k,(s,e) in enumerate(rows):ITEMS.append((s,[options[k]]+[v for j,v in enumerate(options) if j!=k],e))
group(['Something the user knows','Something the user possesses','Something inherent to the user','A contextual risk signal'],[
('A verifier checks a memorized passphrase. Which category describes this evidence?', 'A memorized secret is a knowledge factor.'),
('A user proves control of a registered hardware authenticator by completing its challenge. Which category describes control of that device?', 'Control of the authenticator is a possession factor; the device being known does not make it a knowledge factor.'),
('An authenticator compares a fingerprint with its enrolled template. Which category describes the biometric characteristic?', 'A biometric characteristic is an inherence factor. Its use within an authenticator does not turn it into a memorized secret.'),
('An access service considers an unusual login location in addition to authentication. What is the location observation?', 'Location can influence risk assessment, but is not by itself proof of knowledge, possession or a biometric characteristic.')])
group(['Identity proofing','Authentication','Authorization','Access accounting'],[
('Before issuing a new account, an organization validates evidence linking an applicant to a claimed real-world identity. Which process is this?', 'Identity proofing establishes confidence in the claimed identity before enrollment.'),
('An enrolled employee uses a registered credential to establish control of an existing account. Which process is being performed?', 'Authentication verifies the claimant using an authenticator associated with the account.'),
('A signed-in analyst requests a confidential report. The application evaluates whether the analyst may read that specific report. Which process is this?', 'Authorization decides whether the authenticated principal may perform the requested action.'),
('A service records which account exported a file, when it happened and the result. Which access-management function does the record support?', 'Accounting records activity for attribution and review. It does not itself grant permission.')])
group(['Provision the approved entitlement','Adjust access after a role change','Disable access at separation','Recertify existing access'],[
('A new hire has completed identity checks and the manager has approved a defined job role. Which lifecycle action implements that approval?', 'Provision the approved role and only its required entitlements.'),
('An employee moves from purchasing to support. The old purchasing permissions are no longer required. Which lifecycle action addresses the change?', 'Review and adjust access for the new role, removing obsolete rights rather than accumulating them.'),
('An employee leaves the organization at an agreed time. Which identity lifecycle action must be coordinated with that event?', 'Disable access and associated sessions or credentials under the separation procedure.'),
('A quarterly review asks owners to confirm whether currently assigned permissions remain justified. Which lifecycle activity is this?', 'Recertification evaluates continued need; it is distinct from initial provisioning.')])
group(['Discretionary access control','Mandatory access control','Role-based access control','Attribute-based access control'],[
('Within policy, a file owner decides which colleagues receive permission to the file. Which access model is illustrated?', 'Discretionary control lets an authorized owner determine access to the object.'),
('A system enforces centrally assigned classification labels and clearances that individual file owners cannot override. Which model is illustrated?', 'Mandatory control enforces centrally governed label rules rather than owner discretion.'),
('Permissions are assigned to the payroll-clerk job function, and users receive them through membership in that function. Which model is illustrated?', 'Role-based control assigns permissions through defined roles.'),
('An authorization rule evaluates user department, resource sensitivity and current device compliance together. Which model is illustrated?', 'Attribute-based control evaluates attributes of the subject, object and environment.')])
group(['SAML federation','OAuth delegated authorization','OpenID Connect authentication','Directory group membership'],[
('An enterprise identity provider sends an XML-based assertion to a partner service for federated sign-in. Which mechanism fits this exchange?', 'SAML uses assertions for federation. XML assertions distinguish this case from OpenID Connect.'),
('A user lets a scheduling service access a limited portion of another service through scoped access tokens, without giving it the account password. Which mechanism fits?', 'OAuth supports delegated authorization using scoped access tokens; it does not alone define user authentication.'),
('A client needs a standardized identity layer over OAuth, including an ID token describing the authenticated user. Which mechanism fits?', 'OpenID Connect adds an authentication and identity layer, including ID tokens, to OAuth.'),
('An internal file server grants access because an account belongs to an approved group in the organization’s directory. Which mechanism directly provides this entitlement?', 'Directory group membership maps the account to a local entitlement without implying any federation-token exchange.')])
group(['Validate the token audience','Enforce the token expiry','Verify the trusted issuer','Check the requested permission'],[
('A validly signed token was created for a different application. Which check should prevent this service from accepting it for itself?', 'Audience validation ensures that a token is intended for the receiving service.'),
('A previously valid token is presented after its permitted lifetime. Which check rejects this use?', 'Expiry enforcement limits acceptance beyond the token’s validity window.'),
('A token names an identity provider that this service has never agreed to trust. Which trust check is missing if it is accepted?', 'The service must trust and validate the issuer, not merely accept a structurally valid token.'),
('A legitimate, unexpired token for this service permits reading records but the caller requests deletion. Which check should deny the action?', 'Authorization must compare the requested operation with the granted permissions; token validity alone is insufficient.')])
group(['Reduce standing privilege','Require separate approvals','Constrain the service identity','Review inherited permissions'],[
('Administrators need elevated rights only during approved maintenance windows, but currently retain them all month. Which improvement directly addresses the excess duration?', 'Time-bound elevation reduces standing privilege while supporting authorized work.'),
('One employee can both request and authorize a sensitive payment. Which improvement addresses the conflict between those duties?', 'Separate approval duties reduce the ability of one person to complete conflicting steps unchecked.'),
('A batch job needs to read one queue, yet its machine account can administer the entire platform. Which improvement directly addresses the excessive scope?', 'Limit the service identity to the operations and resources the job actually needs.'),
('A user has no direct permission to a restricted folder but can reach it through nested groups. Which investigation best explains the effective access?', 'Inherited and nested group grants can create effective access beyond direct assignments.')])
group(['Phishing-resistant authentication','Replay-resistant authentication','Password blocklist screening','Account recovery verification'],[
('A service wants cryptographic authentication bound to the legitimate verifier so a lookalike website cannot simply relay a user-entered secret. Which property is required?', 'Verifier-bound cryptographic authentication can resist phishing. A typed one-time code alone is not phishing-resistant.'),
('A captured authentication response must not work again in a later login transaction. Which property addresses this requirement?', 'Replay resistance prevents reuse of a previously accepted response, such as through fresh challenges.'),
('At password enrollment, the system rejects commonly used and known compromised choices. Which safeguard is being applied?', 'Blocklist screening rejects unsuitable password choices at establishment or change.'),
('A caller who lost all authenticators requests a replacement credential. Which process must establish that the caller may regain the account?', 'Recovery verification protects the account when normal authenticators are unavailable; convenience must not bypass identity assurance.')])
group(['Terminate the active sessions','Revoke the delegated grant','Remove the obsolete group grant','Rotate the exposed service secret'],[
('An account has been disabled, but an existing session remains usable because the application has not invalidated it. Which action addresses the remaining access path?', 'Invalidate active sessions as supported by the application; account disabling may not automatically terminate every existing session.'),
('A user no longer wants a third-party application to access their stored documents through an approved delegation. Which permission should be withdrawn?', 'Revoke the delegated authorization grant and associated token access according to the service mechanism.'),
('A transferred employee retains access solely through an old departmental group. Which change removes the identified source of access?', 'Remove the obsolete group grant while preserving justified permissions for the new role.'),
('An application credential was accidentally disclosed in a support attachment. Which credential lifecycle action addresses its continued validity?', 'Replace and revoke the exposed secret through a coordinated rotation process; deleting the attachment alone does not invalidate it.')])
group(['A one-way trust relationship','A bidirectional trust relationship','A transitive trust relationship','Per-request resource authorization'],[
('Domain A accepts identities from domain B, while domain B does not accept identities from A. Which trust description fits?', 'One-way trust permits recognition in one direction only; it does not imply reciprocal trust.'),
('Two domains explicitly accept each other’s identities under the configured agreement. Which trust description fits?', 'A bidirectional relationship permits identity recognition in both directions, subject to separate authorization rules.'),
('A configured trust permits recognition through an intermediate trusted domain rather than requiring a direct agreement between every pair. Which description fits?', 'Transitivity allows trust to extend through defined relationships. It must not be assumed for every trust arrangement.'),
('A system recognizes a partner identity but still evaluates each requested application operation using current policy. Which decision remains separate from identity trust?', 'Recognition of an identity does not automatically authorize every resource operation. Resource authorization remains necessary.')])
assert len(ITEMS)==40
